From eb00d4922ad1206e7752047e172cb432b6df2627 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dimitry B <dimitryB@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 03:16:54 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 16/18] eip155: keep a success whose caller left for that
 caller's retry

Since 0015 a settlement runs to its end when its HTTP client disconnects.
If the transaction was then mined within the receipt wait, the settle ended
in success, the drop guard removed the authorization's record, and nobody
received the success: the next identical retry, and /verify, got
invalid_transaction_state ("already used on chain") with "transaction": "".
The buyer was charged and not served, and the hash was only in the log.

- x402-types: SettleCaller tells a settlement whether its caller is still
  waiting for the outcome. Facilitator::settle_for and
  X402SchemeFacilitator::settle_for take one; their defaults ignore it and
  call settle, so other implementations are unaffected.
- facilitator-local: post_settle passes a SettleCaller that turns false when
  the handler's future is dropped (the client disconnected).
  FacilitatorLocal::settle_for hands it to the scheme handler.
- eip155 (v2 exact): a settlement that succeeds after its caller left keeps
  its record, naming the transaction, exactly like a settlement_pending one.
  The first identical retry claims the success (pending_outcome, atomic
  claim as before); later settles and /verify are replays; any other
  request carrying the authorization still gets invalid_transaction_state.
  A retry whose own caller has left does not claim it. The record still
  expires after 15 minutes. InFlight::pending is renamed InFlight::keep.

A client that disconnects in the instant between the settlement's check and
the response being written still loses that success.
